From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Morinaga <me@gustavomorinaga.dev>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 02:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] spu: a write over a command-buffer park stores the park's
 word last

Killzone HD's PSGL leaves holes in its command ring for SPU jobs: NOPs,
and a self-JUMP (a park) at the start of each 128-byte line. The RSX
walker waits on a park until a job's write lands there. One memcpy makes
the words visible in its own order, and a copy preempted midway leaves
the line half new: a walker that saw the park go could read the old NOPs
or the next line's park in the middle of the new packets, and walk data.

A DMA PUT, PUTLLC or PUTLLUC that covers parks now stores everything
else first, then, after a full fence, the parks' words from the last
line to the first. The bytes are the same; a write with no park in it is
the one memcpy it was. SPU_PUT_PARK_LAST=0 keeps the old copy (A/B);
SPU_PUT_TEAR_MS=<ms> pauses a copy midway (fault injection).

tools/test_spu_put_park_last.py: random writes keep their bytes, and a
thread waiting on the park, as the walker does, never sees a write half
done, also with a copy paused midway; the control (old copy paused
midway) is caught.
